  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A flood risk survey has three parts: exposure (flood zone and source), resilience (PFR measures), and internal escape-of-water risk</li><li>Environment Agency flood zones and Flood Re describe the setting, but a survey confirms what the maps cannot see at property level</li><li>Property flood resilience covers barriers, non-return valves, raised services and resistant materials, in place or recommended</li><li>Escape of water is one of the most common home insurance losses, so internal plumbing risk belongs in the report</li><li>The software captures the survey and generates the report; it does not price the risk or model flood depth, the surveyor and underwriter do</li></ul></div><h2>What a flood risk survey is for</h2><p>A flood risk survey gives an insurer or property owner a grounded view of how a specific property would fare in a flood, and what could reduce the loss. It informs underwriting, premiums, resilience grants and, after a loss, reinstatement. It sits between the broad-brush flood map and the detailed hydraulic model, adding the thing neither has: a person on site, looking.</p><p>Software does not assess the risk. The competent surveyor does. What software does is make the survey easier to record correctly on site, harder to forget a resilience measure, and faster to hand back to the underwriter. A good tool means nobody reconstructs a flood report from memory once the property is two counties behind them.</p><h2>Exposure: flood zones, sources and Flood Re</h2><p>The Environment Agency publishes flood zone mapping for England, with equivalent bodies covering Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland, describing flooding likelihood from rivers and the sea. Surface water, groundwater and drainage flooding add further sources a map alone can understate. Flood Re, the reinsurance scheme that helps make home insurance available in higher-risk areas, sits behind much of the domestic market. Quickler lets the surveyor record the property's setting and the sources they judge relevant, with observations and photos. It is not a flood model and does not calculate depth or probability. Flood zone definitions, mapping and Flood Re change over time, so check the current Environment Agency data and scheme rules.</p><h2>Resilience: property flood resilience measures</h2><p>Property flood resilience, PFR, is the set of measures that keep water out or limit damage: door and airbrick barriers, non-return valves, raised sockets and services, pumps, and resistant flooring and plaster. A resilience-focused survey records which are fitted, maintained and usable, and which are worth recommending. The common failure is a measure that exists on paper but not in practice. Quickler captures each with a photo at the point of observation, so the report shows the barrier was actually present and serviceable, not just specified. The recommendation remains the surveyor's professional judgement.</p><h2>Escape of water: the internal risk</h2><p>External flooding gets the attention, but escape of water, a burst pipe, a leaking appliance, an overflowing tank, is one of the most frequent and costly home insurance losses. A thorough survey notes the plumbing, stopcock locations, loft tanks and appliance connections that are common escape-of-water sources. Quickler lets the surveyor record these alongside the external flood picture, in one report, with photos. Whether a feature materially affects the risk is the surveyor's call; how it affects the premium is the underwriter's.</p><h2>What to look for in a flood survey tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to record findings during the survey, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Resilience evidence:</strong> each PFR measure tied to a photo showing it is present and serviceable, not just specified</li><li><strong>Both water risks in one report:</strong> external flooding and internal escape of water, so the underwriter sees the full picture</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes firms for underwriters and panel surveyors. Quickler charges per report, unlimited users on every bundle</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, surveyor, property, every observation, for underwriting and any later claim</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the survey and generates the report. It does not model flood depth, price the risk, or replace the Environment Agency flood map. It records the evidence a competent surveyor concludes on and an underwriter prices from. The competent surveyor carries the assessment. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a flood risk survey app?</h3><p>It is a tool that helps a surveyor record a flood risk assessment on site and produce a report for an insurer or owner: exposure against flood zones, property flood resilience measures, and internal escape-of-water risk. Options range from paper and spreadsheets, to generic survey apps, to conversation-based tools like Quickler that run the workflow over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>Does a flood survey replace the Environment Agency flood map?</h3><p>No. The flood map describes flood likelihood for an area, not a specific property. A survey confirms property-level detail the map cannot see, such as ground levels, airbricks and drainage. Quickler records the surveyor's site findings against that context; it is not a flood model and does not calculate flood depth. Check the current Environment Agency data.</p><h3>What is property flood resilience?</h3><p>Property flood resilience, PFR, is the set of measures that keep water out or limit damage: barriers, non-return valves, raised sockets and services, pumps and resistant materials. A survey records which are fitted and serviceable and which are worth recommending. Quickler captures each with a photo at the point of observation; the recommendation is the surveyor's professional judgement.</p><h3>Can I run a flood survey over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The surveyor receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/insurance-survey-software-uk.html\">Insurance survey software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/property-risk-assessment-insurance-uk.html\">Property risk assessment for insurance</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/insurance-risk-survey-report-uk.html\">Insurance risk survey report</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/plant-machinery-insurance-inspection-uk.html\">Plant and machinery insurance inspection</a></li></ul>",
